# What a translation unit READ, and whether it still says the same
# thing (M-separate-compilation acceptance row 2's second half; panel
# 093 R4, which names "extern headers' contents" as part of the key).
#
# THE HOLE THIS CLOSES, measured before it was written. A two-module
# program whose `extern "conf.h"` group declares `constant CONF_LIMIT`
# printed `1` at exit 0 with the header on disk saying `2`: the emitted
# C is byte-identical either way — the accessor is `return CONF_LIMIT;`
# — so the per-TU key could not see the edit and the warm object was
# reused. A wrong answer at exit 0 is the worst outcome a build system
# has, and CLAUDE.md §12 puts it ahead of every other criterion here.
#
# WHY CLANG'S OWN LISTING AND NOT THE GROUP'S HEADER NAMES. A header
# includes other headers, so hashing the names an `extern` writes down
# would miss the file one level below — and resolving `<sqlite3.h>` to a
# path means re-implementing the include search. `-MD` makes clang say
# which files it actually opened, transitively, with the paths it
# actually used; that is a fact about the compilation in hand rather
# than a premise about the world (CLAUDE.md §11). It rides the compile
# that is happening anyway: no second clang invocation.
#
# WHY BESIDE THE OBJECT AND NOT IN THE DIRECTORY NAME. The key names
# the directory, and the directory has to be known BEFORE clang runs —
# but the header set is only known after. R4's guarantee is that a
# stale object is never reused, and a recorded digest per dependency
# gives exactly that: the object is served only when every file it read
# still hashes the same. What is given up is directory retention (two
# versions of a header do not keep two objects), which costs a
# recompile and never an answer.
#
# TWO OBJECTS ARE CACHED IN THIS COMPILER AND BOTH USE THIS, because
# the second is the first's adjacent shape and CLAUDE.md §1 says a
# repair is attacked there before it is committed. A translation unit's
# object is the case above. The **runtime's** object is keyed on the
# runtime's own sources and on nothing else, so `runtime.c` including
# `<stdio.h>` had the identical hole one level over — and its failure
# is the loudest kind there is: every TU recompiles against the new
# headers while the runtime object does not, which is a libc ABI
# mismatch inside one binary. Nobody had to change a line of Heroes for
# that to happen.
#
# AND IT CATCHES A CLANG UPGRADE, which was not the point and is worth
# writing down. `Toolchain.fingerprint` is the HEROES version (main.hero's
# `VERSION`) rather than clang's, so a new clang alone never moved the key. Its
# builtin headers live under a versioned path
# (`.../clang/21/include/stdbool.h`), which every listing records, so a
# major-version bump makes that path vanish, `digest_of` answers
# `absent`, and everything rebuilds. That is a side effect of asking
# the right question, not a design — a clang whose headers keep their
# path and change their contents is caught by the digest instead, and
# one that changes neither cannot matter.
#
# **The paragraph above holds for an UPGRADE and not for a SWITCH** (defect
# 122, 2026-09-28). With two clangs installed side by side, Apple's 21 and
# Homebrew's 22.1.8 on one Mac, choosing the other one on the `PATH` removes
# no header: the old listing's `.../clang/21/include/` files are all still
# there and still hash the same, so every object stayed fresh, and a program
# printing `__clang_major__` printed 21 under clang 22 (panel 182's critic,
# then the coordinator's reproducer). And the one that "cannot matter" does:
# a flag added to `flags.hero` changes no header either. Neither is this
# file's question, which is what a compile READ; both are the key's, and the
# key now carries the compiler and its flags (`Toolchain.compiler_key`).
#
# THE FALLBACKS ALL POINT THE LOUD WAY (CLAUDE.md §11). No record, an
# unreadable record, a short line, a vanished header: every one of them
# is "not fresh", which recompiles. Nothing here can turn a miss into a
# hit.
#
# **THE RECORD IS A POINTER, AND IT NAMES WHAT IT VOUCHES FOR** (defect 134).
# It stood beside `<m>.o` and was written before the object was renamed into
# place, so the record of a NEWER compile could stand beside an OLDER object:
# measured, a record copied in as a build killed between the two would leave
# it, and the program printed 7 at exit 0 with its header saying 8. Two
# builds of one key publishing the two files in turn left the same pair. So
# the record's first line is the digest of its own rows, every artifact of
# that compile is named by it (`<m>-<digest>.o`), and the record is published
# LAST: whichever record a reader finds, the object it names was compiled
# from exactly the files it lists, and a record whose object is not there is
# a recompile. One line per dependency after it, `<digest> <path>`.

use cli/digest
use cli/process

# One file's content digest, computed at most once per build.
#
# The memo is what makes this affordable: the 39 headers a trivial
# program reaches are 797 KB, and 157 translation units sharing them
# would be 125 MB of hashing per warm build (measured at ~1.2 MB/s,
# which is 104 s). Read once, hashed once, answered 157 times.
#
# A file that cannot be read answers `absent` rather than a digest, and
# that is a valid answer rather than an error: a header that vanished
# must invalidate the object. It cannot collide with a real digest,
# which is always sixteen hex characters.
function digest_of(path: str, @memo: {str: str}) -> str
    known = memo[path]

    if !known.is_err()
        return known.must()
    text = read_file(path)
    answer: str @ "absent"

    if !text.is_err()
        answer @ digest.digest(text.must())
    memo[path] @ answer
    return answer

# Every path in one of clang's Make-style dependency files.
#
# The shape, with `-MT dep` so the target is a word we chose rather
# than a path we would have to un-parse:
#
#     dep: /a/tu.c /usr/include/stdio.h \
#       /usr/include/sys/cdefs.h
#
# Two escapes matter and both are read as bytes rather than guessed at:
# `\` at the end of a line folds the list, and `\ ` is a literal space
# inside a path — macOS puts them in SDK paths. Everything up to the
# first `:` is the target and is dropped.
function paths_of(text: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    # The path being read, in the runs between its escapes.
    piece: [str] @ []
    start: i64 @ 0
    index: i64 @ 0
    seen_target: bool @ false

    while index < text.len() && !seen_target
        if text[index] == 58
            seen_target @ true
        index @ index + 1

    start @ index

    while index < text.len()
        byte = text[index]

        if byte == 92 && index + 1 < text.len() && text[index + 1] == 32
            run = text.slice(from: start, to: index)
            piece @ piece.push(run)
            piece @ piece.push(" ")
            index @ index + 2
            start @ index
            continue

        # `\` followed by a line end is make's fold; followed by anything
        # else it is a BYTE OF THE PATH — which is every separator in
        # `C:\Program Files\LLVM\…`. This branch treated a bare backslash
        # as a break until 2026-08-31, and the measured cost was a cache
        # that never hit on Windows: clang's depfile paths shattered at
        # every `\` into fragments (`C:`, `Program Files`, `LLVM`, …), each
        # digest came back `absent`, and `fresh()` refused every record —
        # three objects rebuilt on a build where nothing was edited, every
        # build, exactly what `cache/an untouched build still hits` exists
        # to catch.
        if byte == 92 && index + 1 < text.len() && text[index + 1] != 10 && text[index + 1] != 13
            index @ index + 1
            continue

        if byte == 92 || byte == 32 || byte == 9 || byte == 10 || byte == 13
            run = text.slice(from: start, to: index)
            piece @ piece.push(run)
            path = piece.join("")
            piece @ []

            if path.len() > 0
                out @ out.push(path)
            index @ index + 1
            start @ index
            continue
        index @ index + 1

    run = text.slice(from: start, to: text.len())
    piece @ piece.push(run)
    path = piece.join("")

    if path.len() > 0
        out @ out.push(path)
    return out

# The rows of a record, from the dependency listing of a finished compile.
#
# **`skip` names what the KEY already covers**, and the caller says what
# that is because only the caller knows. It is asked of the path rather
# than assumed of a position in the listing. Both callers have one:
# a translation unit's `.c` lives in the directory the key names, so it
# cannot change without the object landing somewhere else entirely
# (~90 KB per module — recording it would hash 14 MB per build of this
# compiler to re-answer a question the directory name settled); the
# runtime's own sources are hashed whole into `Toolchain.runtime_key`.
#
# A path holding a newline would write a line the reader cannot parse.
# That is safe by construction rather than by luck: the reader treats
# anything it cannot parse as not fresh, so such an object is rebuilt
# every time instead of being served stale.
function rows_of(depfile: str, skip: str, @memo: {str: str}) -> str?
    listing = read_file(depfile)

    if listing.is_err()
        return fail(code: "failed", msg: "clang wrote no dependency listing at " + depfile)
    rows: [str] @ []

    for path in paths_of(listing.must())
        if under(path: path, prefix: skip)
            continue
        rows @ rows.push(digest_of(path, @memo) + " " + path)

    return ok(rows.join("\n"))

# The whole record for those rows: the digest its artifacts are named by,
# then the rows. The caller publishes it after every artifact it names.
function pointer_text(rows: str) -> str
    return digest.digest(rows) + "\n" + rows

# The digest the artifacts a record vouches for are named by, or absent
# where there is no record, one this format did not write, or one whose
# files no longer say what they said. The first line is read as written:
# the record was published whole, by one writer who digested these rows.
function vouched(record_path: str, @memo: {str: str}) -> str?
    kept = read_file(record_path)

    if kept.is_err()
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "no record at " + record_path)
    text = kept.must()

    # Sixteen digits and a line end, checked by bytes before anything is
    # sliced, for `fresh_rows`'s reason below.
    if text.len() < 17 || text[16] != 10 || !is_digest(text: text, length: 16)
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a record this compiler writes")

    if !fresh_rows(text.slice(from: 17, to: text.len()), @memo)
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "stale")
    return ok(text.slice(from: 0, to: 16))

# Whether the first `length` bytes are lowercase hex, the only bytes a
# digest is spelt in.
function is_digest(text: str, length: i64) -> bool
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < length
        b = text[at]

        if !(b >= '0' && b <= '9') && !(b >= 'a' && b <= 'f')
            return false
        at @ at + 1

    return true

# Prefix test by BYTES, never by slicing: a path is arbitrary UTF-8 and
# `slice` aborts where it would split a character (spec § 10 Strings, arrays, maps), so cutting
# a path at the prefix's length is a crash waiting for a directory with
# an accent in its name. Comparing bytes cannot abort.
function under(path: str, prefix: str) -> bool
    if path.len() < prefix.len()
        return false
    index: i64 @ 0

    while index < prefix.len()
        if path[index] != prefix[index]
            return false
        index @ index + 1

    return true

# Whether every file a record's rows name still says the same thing. False
# means recompile.
function fresh_rows(rows: str, @memo: {str: str}) -> bool
    for row in lines(rows)
        if row.len() == 0
            continue

        # `<16 hex><space><path>`, and the SPACE is checked before
        # anything is sliced. Not tidiness: `slice` aborts where it
        # would split a character (spec § 10 Strings, arrays, maps), so cutting at byte 17 of
        # a line this format did not write is a crash. A space at 16
        # makes 16 and 17 character boundaries, because a space is one
        # whole character and the byte after it begins the next.
        # The line that can reach here unrecognised is a path holding a
        # newline, which `lines` splits in two — and this sends it to
        # the loud side: not fresh, recompile.
        if row.len() < 18 || row[16] != 32
            return false

        if digest_of(row.slice(from: 17, to: row.len()), @memo) != row.slice(from: 0, to: 16)
            return false

    return true

function lines(text: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    start: i64 @ 0
    index: i64 @ 0

    while index < text.len()
        if text[index] == 10
            out @ out.push(text.slice(from: start, to: index))
            start @ index + 1
        index @ index + 1

    out @ out.push(text.slice(from: start, to: text.len()))
    return out

test "a Windows depfile keeps its backslashes and its escaped spaces"
    # clang targeting msvc writes exactly this shape, and the old walk split
    # it at every backslash: the first fragment in the broken record was the
    # bare drive letter `C:`, measured 2026-08-31 on the VPS.
    got = paths_of("dep: C:\\Program\\ Files\\LLVM\\lib\\clang\\22\\include\\stddef.h D:\\w\\x.h\n")
    assert got.len() == 2
    assert got[0] == "C:\\Program Files\\LLVM\\lib\\clang\\22\\include\\stddef.h"
    assert got[1] == "D:\\w\\x.h"

    # And the fold is still a fold on both line endings.
    folded = paths_of("dep: a.h \\\n b.h\n")
    assert folded == ["a.h", "b.h"]

test "a dependency listing gives up its paths, target dropped"
    got = paths_of("dep: /a/tu.c /usr/include/stdio.h\n")
    assert got.len() == 2
    assert got[0] == "/a/tu.c"
    assert got[1] == "/usr/include/stdio.h"

test "a folded listing is one list"
    got = paths_of("dep: /a.h \\\n  /b.h \\\n  /c.h\n")
    assert got.len() == 3
    assert got[2] == "/c.h"

test "an escaped space stays inside its path"
    got = paths_of("dep: /Applications/Xcode\\ 16.app/x.h /b.h\n")
    assert got.len() == 2
    assert got[0] == "/Applications/Xcode 16.app/x.h"
    assert got[1] == "/b.h"

test "an empty listing has no paths"
    assert paths_of("").len() == 0
    assert paths_of("dep:\n").len() == 0

test "a file that is not there answers absent, not a digest"
    memo: {str: str} @ {}
    assert digest_of("selfhost/no-such-file-exists.h", @memo) == "absent"
    assert memo.len() == 1

test "a digest is asked of the file once"
    memo: {str: str} @ {}
    first = digest_of("runtime/heroes_runtime.h", @memo)
    assert first.len() == 16
    assert digest_of("runtime/heroes_runtime.h", @memo) == first
    assert memo.len() == 1

test "no record vouches for nothing"
    memo: {str: str} @ {}
    assert vouched("selfhost/no-such-directory/deps.txt", @memo).is_err()

# The invariant itself, made to fire (CLAUDE.md §9): a record written
# over a file vouches for its artifacts, and stops the moment the file's
# bytes change. Nothing here goes through clang — the dependency
# listing is hand-written, because what is under test is the record.
test "a record goes stale when what it recorded changes, and names its artifacts by its own rows"
    dir = "build/selftest-deps"
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()

    # OUTSIDE `dir` on purpose: what lives under the key's own
    # directory is deliberately not recorded, so a watched file placed
    # in there would make this case pass for the wrong reason — which
    # is exactly what it did when this test was first written.
    watched = "build/selftest-deps-watched.h"
    assert !write_file(path: watched, text: "#define LIMIT 1\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/listing.d", text: "dep: " + watched + "\n").is_err()
    writing: {str: str} @ {}
    rows = rows_of(depfile: dir + "/listing.d", skip: dir + "/", @writing).must()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/deps.txt", text: pointer_text(rows)).is_err()
    reading: {str: str} @ {}
    first = vouched(dir + "/deps.txt", @reading).must()
    assert first == digest.digest(rows)

    # Another header, another digest: the second compile's artifacts are
    # other names, so the first compile's object is never the second's.
    assert !write_file(path: watched, text: "#define LIMIT 2\n").is_err()
    after: {str: str} @ {}
    assert vouched(dir + "/deps.txt", @after).is_err()
    again: {str: str} @ {}
    second = digest.digest(rows_of(depfile: dir + "/listing.d", skip: dir + "/", @again).must())
    assert second != first

test "a prefix test on a path with an accent in it does not abort"
    assert under(path: "build/tu-1/x.c", prefix: "build/tu-1/")
    assert !under(path: "build/tu-2/x.c", prefix: "build/tu-1/")
    assert !under(path: "/a", prefix: "/abc")

    # A genuine prefix that stops mid-`à`, and two paths that differ
    # INSIDE a multi-byte character. Cutting either with `slice` at the
    # prefix's length would abort (spec § 10 Strings, arrays, maps); comparing bytes answers.
    assert under(path: "/Cartellà/x.h", prefix: "/Cartel")
    assert !under(path: "/Cartellá/x.h", prefix: "/Cartellà/")

test "what the key already names is not recorded"
    dir = "build/selftest-deps-own"
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/watched.h", text: "#define X 1\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/listing.d", text: "dep: " + dir + "/unit.c " + dir + "/watched.h\n").is_err()
    memo: {str: str} @ {}

    # Both paths are inside the keyed directory, so the rows are empty
    # and the memo was never asked about either; the record still vouches.
    rows = rows_of(depfile: dir + "/listing.d", skip: dir + "/", @memo).must()
    assert rows.len() == 0
    assert memo.len() == 0
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/deps.txt", text: pointer_text(rows)).is_err()
    assert vouched(dir + "/deps.txt", @memo).must() == digest.digest("")

test "a record this format did not write vouches for nothing, and does not abort"
    dir = "build/selftest-deps-shape"
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    memo: {str: str} @ {}

    # A path with a newline in it, which is what `lines` would hand
    # back in two pieces — the second piece has no digest and no space
    # at byte 16, and slicing it at 17 would split the `à`.
    assert !fresh_rows("0123456789abcdef /a/b.h\nCartellà-continued-name.h\n", @memo)
    assert !fresh_rows("short\n", @memo)

    # A first line that is not a digest, a record cut before its line
    # end, an empty file, and the shape of the record this replaced.
    for text in ["0123456789abcdeZ\n", "0123456789abcdef", "", "0123456789abcdef runtime/heroes_runtime.h\n", "àààààààààààààààààà\n"]
        assert !write_file(path: dir + "/deps.txt", text: text).is_err()
        assert vouched(dir + "/deps.txt", @memo).is_err()

test "text splits into lines, the last one without a newline"
    got = lines("a\nbb\nccc")
    assert got.len() == 3
    assert got[1] == "bb"
